Ollagraph managed proxy + browser | Managed proxy + browser | Yes | Very high | $0.020/request | Enterprise anti-bot evasion</code></pre>

<h3>When to Use Each Alternative</h3>
<p><strong>Roll your own with requests + Playwright:</strong> Best for teams with dedicated infrastructure and the engineering bandwidth to maintain two pipelines. You get full control but pay in operational complexity.</p>
<p><strong>Ollagraph smart fetch:</strong> Best for teams that want a single API endpoint that handles the decision automatically. You send a URL, get back structured data, and never think about rendering strategy. The smart classifier runs in under 50 ms and escalates only when needed.</p>
<p><strong>Managed browser APIs:</strong> Best for small-scale projects where the per-request cost is acceptable and you want zero infrastructure management. At scale, the per-request pricing becomes uneconomical compared to self-hosted hybrid approaches.</p>

<h2>13. Enterprise / Cloud Deployment</h2>

<h3>Scaling a Hybrid Pipeline</h3>
<p>At enterprise scale, the hybrid approach requires careful capacity planning. Here is how we structure it at Ollagraph:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Static fetch tier:</strong> A pool of lightweight workers (2 CPU, 4 GB RAM) behind a load balancer. Each worker handles hundreds of concurrent requests using async HTTP clients. This tier handles 80%+ of traffic.</li>
  <li><strong>Headless browser tier:</strong> A pool of browser-hosting instances (8 CPU, 32 GB RAM) running Playwright in a containerized environment. Each instance hosts 8–12 concurrent browser contexts. This tier handles the 20% of traffic that requires JS rendering.</li>
  <li><strong>Classifier tier:</strong> A lightweight service that inspects static responses and routes requests to the appropriate tier. The classifier itself is stateless and runs on minimal compute — it is essentially a set of regex checks and heuristic rules.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Observability</h3>
<p>Track these metrics in production:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Static-to-headless ratio:</strong> The percentage of requests that escalate. Sudden changes indicate site migrations or classification drift.</li>
  <li><strong>Browser launch latency:</strong> P50 and P95 time to acquire a warm browser context. Spikes indicate pool exhaustion.</li>
  <li><strong>Extraction success rate by strategy:</strong> If headless extraction success drops below 90%, investigate anti-bot changes on target sites.</li>
  <li><strong>Cost per successful extraction:</strong> The true north metric. A hybrid pipeline should cost 5–10× less per successful extraction than an all-headless pipeline.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Multi-Tenant Isolation</h3>
<p>If your pipeline serves multiple teams or clients, isolate browser contexts at the tenant level. A compromised page from one tenant should not affect another tenant’s sessions. Use separate browser pools or at minimum separate browser contexts with distinct cookie jars and localStorage.</p>

<h2>14. FAQs</h2>

<h3>Q1. What is the main difference between static fetch and a headless browser?</h3>
<p>Static fetch sends an HTTP request and reads the raw HTML response. No JavaScript runs, no CSS renders, no dynamic content loads. A headless browser launches a full Chromium or Firefox engine, executes all JavaScript, waits for network requests to settle, and returns the fully rendered DOM. The difference is whether you get the server’s initial HTML or the page as a user would see it after all scripts finish.</p>

<h2>Q2. When should I use static fetch instead of a headless browser?</h2>
<p>Use static fetch when the page is server-rendered or pre-built static HTML. Common examples include WordPress blogs, documentation sites (Docusaurus, GitBook), news sites, and most government or institutional pages. The telltale sign: if curl returns a complete page with visible text, titles, and content, static fetch will work. If you see mostly &lt;script&gt; tags and empty divs, you need a browser.</p>

<h2>Q3. When is a headless browser absolutely required?</h2>
<p>A headless browser is required for single-page applications built with React, Vue, Angular, or SvelteKit. These frameworks ship an empty HTML shell and populate content via JavaScript. Also required for pages with infinite scroll, JS-driven authentication, real-time dashboards, and sites protected by anti-bot challenges that require JavaScript execution.</p>

<h2>Q4. How much more expensive is a headless browser compared to static fetch?</h2>
<p>A headless browser costs 50–250× more per request. Static fetch runs at roughly $0.00002 per request in serverless compute. A headless browser with a warm pool costs $0.001–$0.005 per request. Cold starts push that to $0.005–$0.01. At 10 million requests per month, the difference is $200 (static) vs $10,000–$50,000 (headless). That gap is why the hybrid approach exists.</p>

<h2>Q5. Can I detect whether a page needs JavaScript rendering without loading it in a browser?</h2>
<p>Yes. The static HTML contains strong signals: empty &lt;div id="root"&gt; or &lt;div id="app"&gt; mount points, known SPA framework identifiers in &lt;script&gt; source URLs, a high ratio of script bytes to visible text bytes, and missing meta tags or title elements. These heuristics catch roughly 95% of JS-dependent pages with sub-50 ms analysis time.</p>

<h2>Q6. What is a hybrid fetch strategy and why should I use one?</h2>
<p>A hybrid strategy tries static fetch first, analyzes the response for JS-dependency signals, and escalates to a headless browser only when needed. This captures the speed and low cost of static fetch for the majority of pages while falling back to full rendering for SPAs. Most production pipelines save 60–80% on infrastructure costs compared to an all-headless approach. The classifier runs in under 50 ms, so the overhead is negligible.</p>

<h2>Q7. Does Ollagraph support hybrid fetch?</h2>
<p>Yes. Ollagraph's /v1/scrape/smart endpoint implements a hybrid fetch strategy. It sends a static HTTP request, runs a classifier on the response, and automatically escalates to a headless browser if the page requires JavaScript rendering. You get one endpoint that handles both cases. The classifier runs in under 50 ms and the escalation is transparent to your code.</p>

<h2>Q8. How do anti-bot systems affect headless browser success rates?</h2>
<p>Anti-bot systems detect headless browsers through WebDriver flags, missing or inconsistent navigator properties, abnormal canvas fingerprinting results, and unusual WebGL renderer strings. Even with stealth plugins, success rates on heavily protected sites top out around 70–80%. Residential proxies and browser fingerprint rotation improve this but add cost and complexity. Some sites are simply unreachable by automated browsers.</p>

<h2>Q9. What happens if my static fetch returns partial data?</h2>
<p>Partial data is the most dangerous failure mode because it does not look like a failure. The page appears to load, your selectors find some elements, and the pipeline continues producing records — but key fields are missing or contain placeholder values. Always validate extracted data against your expected schema and compare static vs rendered output periodically for a sample of your target sites.</p>

<h2>Q10. Can I use the same selectors for static fetch and headless browser output?</h2>
<p>Not always. Static HTML may use different class names, ID attributes, or DOM structure than the rendered page. CSS-in-JS libraries generate dynamic class names at runtime. Server-rendered markup and client-rendered markup can differ significantly even for the same component. Maintain separate selector sets for each strategy, or use content-based extraction (LLM-powered) that is resilient to structural changes. We have seen cases where the same selector returns completely different elements depending on whether JS ran.</p>

<h2>Q11. How do I handle infinite scroll pages?</h2>
<p>Infinite scroll pages load content incrementally as the user scrolls. A static fetch gets only the initial batch. A headless browser must programmatically scroll the page and wait for new content to appear. In Playwright, use page.evaluate() to scroll to the bottom repeatedly until no new content loads, or until a maximum scroll count is reached. Set a cap — typically 5–10 scrolls — to prevent unbounded execution.</p>

<h2>Q12. What is the best strategy for crawling thousands of sites per hour?</h2>
<p>Use a hybrid approach with domain-level classification caching. Fetch each domain once with a headless browser to classify it, cache the result, and then use static fetch for subsequent pages on the same domain if the site is server-rendered. For SPA domains, route all requests through the headless browser pool. This minimizes classification overhead while maintaining high extraction accuracy.</p>

<h2>15. Conclusion</h2>
<p>The static fetch vs headless browser decision is not a one-time architectural choice. It is a per-request optimization that directly impacts your pipeline's speed, cost, and reliability. Static fetch is fast and cheap but fails on the 60% of the web that relies on JavaScript rendering. Headless browsers are comprehensive but expensive — 50–250× the cost per request.</p>
<p>The winning strategy for most teams is hybrid: default to static, detect when it is insufficient, and escalate to a headless browser only when the signals say so. This approach captures the best of both worlds and typically saves 60–80% on infrastructure costs compared to an all-headless pipeline.</p>
